{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>Add your own functions, filters and tests when you create the environment. They are plain JavaScript functions, and they can be async.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
const kiln = createKiln({
	loader,
	functions: {
		document: (collection: string, query: { slug: string }) =>
			db.find(collection, query),
	},
	filters: {
		readingTime: (text: string) => Math.ceil(text.split(" ").length / 200),
	},
	tests: {
		external: (url: string) => !url.startsWith("/"),
	},
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	{% apply highlight("kiln") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% set post = document("post", slug: route.slug) %}
<p>{{ post.body|readingTime }} min read</p>
{% if post.link is external %}<a href="{{ post.link }}" rel="noopener">Visit</a>{% endif %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>How arguments arrive</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>A filter gets the filtered value first, then its arguments.</li>
		<li>Named arguments arrive as one plain object at the end. <code>document("post", slug: "hello")</code> calls <code>document("post", { slug: "hello" })</code>.</li>
		<li>Arrays arrive as arrays and hashes as plain objects.</li>
		<li>Arrow functions arrive as plain functions. They return a promise if their body calls something async.</li>
		<li>A test should return a boolean, or a promise of one.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Names</h2>

	<ul>
		<li>Names start with a letter or underscore, followed by letters, digits or underscores. Test names can be two words, such as <code>divisible by</code>.</li>
		<li>Functions, filters and tests have separate names, so a filter and a function can share one.</li>
		<li>Your own function, filter or test replaces a built-in one with the same name. The exceptions are <code>parent</code>, <code>block</code>, <code>default</code>, <code>raw</code>, <code>escape</code> and <code>defined</code>, which Kiln reserves.</li>
		<li><code>builtins</code> lists Kiln's own function, filter and test names in alphabetical order, reserved ones included. Check it when a name shouldn't replace a built-in one, such as names that come from plugins: <code>builtins.filters.includes("upper")</code>.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Async functions</h2>

	<p>Any function, filter or test can return a promise. Kiln waits for it where it is called and carries on. See <a href="/docs/language/async-data/">Async data</a>.</p>

	<p>Throwing an error stops the render. The render error's <code>cause</code> is your error, so you can throw your own error types to signal a 404 or a redirect and catch them in your server.</p>

	<h2>Request data</h2>

	<p>Some functions need data about the current request, such as the visitor's language or whether they are previewing drafts. Wrap them in <code>withRender</code>, and pass the data as <code>state</code> when you render.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { createKiln, withRender } from "@lucidkiln/core";

type State = { locale: string; preview: boolean };

const kiln = createKiln<State>({
	loader,
	functions: {
		document: withRender((render, collection: string, query: { slug: string }) =>
			db.find(collection, {
				...query,
				locale: render.state.locale,
				drafts: render.state.preview,
			}),
		),
	},
});

await kiln.render("pages/page", context, {
	state: { locale: "en", preview: false },
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>The first argument describes the render:</p>

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>state</code></td><td>The <code>state</code> passed to <code>render</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>locale</code></td><td>The render's locale</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>timeZone</code></td><td>The render's time zone</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>template</code></td><td>The name of the template making the call</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>signal</code></td><td>The render's abort signal, if there is one</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>tagCache(...tags)</code></td><td>Tags the cached section the call runs in, so your store can clear it when that data changes. See <a href="/docs/tags/cache/#tags">Tags</a></td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>With a <code>State</code> type, TypeScript makes <code>state</code> a required render option, so you can't forget it.</p>

	<h2>Globals</h2>

	<p>Globals are variables every template can read, including included templates, components and macros. Use them for data that is the same on every page, such as the site's name.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
const kiln = createKiln({
	loader,
	globals: { site: { name: "Acme", url: "https://acme.example" } },
});
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Templates can't assign to globals. Values are not awaited, so pass data that is ready, or a function.</p>

	<p>To change a global for one render, such as the locale of the content, pass it in the <a href="/docs/api/rendering/#globals-for-one-render"><code>globals</code> render option</a>.</p>
{% endblock %}
